  "live_records": {
    "q-336699": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336699",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$5{e}^{-t}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-({e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -3{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
