    "q-337280": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337280",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"60px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"60px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{17}{6}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-3{e}^{4t})-3{e}^{4t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
