    "q-337288": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337288",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">.</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6t-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-2t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 3{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t)+6(t-1) \\\\ 6(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4t-5 \\\\ 6t-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
